11.text:
PRIVATE REFLECTION: {
The group consensus remains strong that the clinical presentation describes an elderly patient receiving antiepileptic therapy (valproate, topiramate, and lamotrigine) resulting in Stevens-Johnson syndrome / toxic epidermal necrolysis. Deliberation centers on whether the initiating biochemical reaction is the pharmacokinetic inhibition of Phase II glucuronidation (via UDP-glucuronosyltransferase) by valproate or the subsequent Phase I cytochrome P450-mediated oxidation/epoxidation forming reactive arene oxide intermediates that trigger keratinocyte apoptosis. Both mechanisms are causally linked to blister formation, with glucuronidation serving as the upstream enzymatic trigger and CYP-mediated epoxidation acting as the direct cellular bioactivation reaction.
}

