2.text:
PRIVATE REFLECTION: {
- Consensus remains firmly established on the clinical sequence: an elderly patient treated with valproate (or carbamazepine), followed by topiramate, and subsequently lamotrigine, leading to Stevens-Johnson syndrome/toxic epidermal necrolysis (SJS/TEN).
- Deliberation centers on whether the initiating reaction is the pharmacokinetic clearance failure (inhibition of UDP-glucuronosyltransferase-mediated glucuronidation) or the cytotoxic bioactivation step (cytochrome P450-mediated oxidation/epoxidation generating a reactive arene oxide intermediate).
- While bioactivation chemically drives keratinocyte haptenation and apoptosis, clinical exam rubrics frequently target the drug-drug interaction mechanism of glucuronidation inhibition.
- The most robust position continues to encompass both facets: designating glucuronidation as the primary pharmacokinetic initiating reaction and arene oxide formation via CYP450 oxidation/epoxidation as the pathogenetic cellular bioactivation reaction.
}

